<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Health Conditions" articleSchema={true} datePublished="2026-01-15" dateModified="2026-08-19" description="Browse acupressure treatment guides for common health conditions. Step-by-step protocols for nausea, anxiety, headaches, back pain, and more." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }]}>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Health Conditions</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-4">Targeted acupressure protocols for common health conditions. Each guide recommends specific pressure points and provides step-by-step treatment instructions you can use at home.</p><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-10">From tension headaches to chronic anxiety, acupressure offers a drug-free way to manage symptoms by stimulating the body's natural healing response. The protocols below are based on traditional Chinese medicine principles, with the research support for each condition described honestly on its guide page. Select a condition below to find the most effective points and techniques for relief.</p>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What You'll Find in This Section</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Each guide below covers a specific health condition with the most relevant pressure points, their locations, recommended hold times, and a suggested routine. The strongest research support on this site is for nausea, where PC6 (Neiguan) on the inner forearm is backed by a long-running Cochrane systematic review, and for anxiety, where a systematic review and meta-analysis from 2022 found the overall direction of the evidence positive while calling for more high-quality trials.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">For headaches, a randomized trial from 2010 (Hsieh et al., Am J Chin Med) found one month of acupressure more effective than a muscle relaxant for chronic headache, though reviews describe the headache evidence as promising but limited in quality. For sleep, a randomized trial from 2020 (Liao et al., J Am Med Dir Assoc) found acupressure improved sleep quality in nursing-home residents; that evidence is promising but still preliminary. Evidence for back pain, neck pain, migraine, and constipation is more limited, but the guides still offer practical point-by-point routines used in clinical TCM practice.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Whatever the condition, the guides rely on a handful of well-documented points, such as BL40 (Weizhong) behind the knee for lower back pain, GB20 (Fengchi) at the base of the skull for headaches, and ST36 (Zusanli) below the knee for digestive complaints, with hold times of 30 seconds to 3 minutes per point.</p>
    </section>
